      "id": "the-demon-haunted-world-ch-14",
      "order": 14,
      "chapterNumber": 14,
      "headline": "Antiscience",
      "keyInsight": "Pushing science away while using modern gadgets is like cursing your plumber while taking a hot shower. People often attack scientific thinking because honest doubt feels scary. Superstition gives quick answers with zero homework. Honest testing demands effort, patience, and admitting when we are dead wrong. That bruised ego is why anti-science movements gain ground so quickly. But rejecting clear thinking does not shield us from danger. It merely turns off our headlights while we barrel down a winding mountain road at midnight.",
      "content": "When people get overwhelmed by change, they often look for someone to blame. Science frequently becomes that scapegoat. Critics complain that rational methods drain the poetry from life, treating questions like cold machine parts. But that complaint misses the target completely.\n\nRejecting careful testing is like firing your mechanic because you dislike repair bills. The weird noises under your car hood do not vanish just because you turn up the radio. Ignoring how things work only guarantees a much bigger breakdown tomorrow.\n\nScience does not claim to know everything. Its real genius lies in constantly correcting its own errors. When a society abandons this self-correcting tool, superstition rushes in to fill the empty room. We end up trading hard-won truths for cheap magical charms.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Ignoring evidence never makes danger vanish; it just blindfolds you before the impact.",
