      "id": "the-demon-haunted-world-ch-15",
      "order": 15,
      "chapterNumber": 15,
      "headline": "Newton's Sleep",
      "keyInsight": "The poet William Blake warned against a cold, lifeless world where wonder gets murdered by dull math. He called this trap 'Newton's Sleep.' Many people worry that dissecting a rainbow ruins its beauty. Sagan flips that fear on its head. Understanding the recipe of a sunset does not spoil the view; it adds rich new layers of awe. A master chef appreciates a rich stew far more than someone who can barely boil water. Knowing the physics of light turns a pretty streak of color into a cosmic masterpiece.",
      "content": "Some folks worry that scientific curiosity robs life of magic. They fear that measuring nature turns our world into a gray, soulless warehouse. If you explain why stars shine, does the night sky lose its wonder? Sagan answers with a resounding no.\n\nThink about learning how to play a musical instrument. Practicing scales and reading sheet music might seem mechanical at first. Yet that technical mastery opens the door to playing breathtaking symphonies. Far from choking emotion, knowledge gives it deeper wings.\n\nTrue wonder blooms when our eyes open wide, not when we shut them tight. Seeing how gravity guides oceans and holds galaxies together is vastly more thrilling than ancient ghost stories. Knowledge does not put mystery to sleep. It wakes our minds up to the grandeur of the universe.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Understanding how the world works never kills beauty; it unlocks a deeper level of awe.",
