      "id": "the-demon-haunted-world-ch-16",
      "order": 16,
      "chapterNumber": 16,
      "headline": "When Scientists Unleash Frankenstein",
      "keyInsight": "We love treating scientific inventions like miraculous kitchen gadgets. You plug in the microwave, and dinner is ready in minutes. But every power tool cuts both ways. The exact same chemistry that fertilizes farmland can also level a city block. For a long time, researchers pretended their job ended inside the clean walls of the lab. They claimed the messy fallout was somebody else's problem. That excuse is dead. If you brew a wildly potent chemical, you do not get to walk away when the fumes escape into the neighborhood. Knowledge without a conscience is not pure discovery. It is reckless negligence on a planetary scale.",
      "content": "Imagine designing an ultralight race car with a jet engine. It breaks every speed record on earth. But you deliberately forgot to install brakes, airbags, or a steering wheel. That is what raw technological genius looks like without moral foresight. When the creators of the atomic bomb saw the first mushroom cloud rise over the desert, their pride turned to ash. They realized they had handed civilization matches while standing inside a fireworks warehouse.\n\nScience is not a holy temple separated from everyday life. It is funded by taxpayers, directed by politicians, and deployed by military generals. When researchers claim they only search for abstract truth, they dodge their biggest duty. If you cook a spicy meal, you cannot blame the diner for coughing when you dumped pepper into the pot. Scientists must forecast the side effects of their own discoveries before the storm hits.\n\nThink of genetic editing and artificial intelligence today. They offer dazzling cures for stubborn illnesses, but they also bring real perils. We cannot simply lock the laboratory doors and ban curiosity. Instead, thinkers must stay glued to the steering wheel of the machines they build. Real genius does not just ask if something is possible. It insists on asking whether it is wise.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Inventing incredible tools means nothing if you refuse to take responsibility for how they get used.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Brilliant tools can build a home or burn it to the ground. True science demands taking full responsibility for the fire you kindle.",
